've been very  interested in in terms of molecular  heterogeneity or brain metastasis so  brain metastases are a huge clinical  problem in many diseases but they're a  particular problem in melanoma and  indeed among all solid cancers melanoma  actually has the highest risk of CNS  metastasis with up to 70% of patients  showing evidence of CNS involvement on  autopsy studies historically these  patients have had very poor outcomes  with a median overall survival of  approximately four months from diagnosis  and those outcomes are improving with  our contemporary targeted therapies and  immune therapies however we're  consistently seeing that those  treatments do not work as well in  patients with brain metastases and in  the brain metastases themselves as we  see in extracranial tumors and indeed  the brain is a common initial site of  relapse for both targeted enemy in  therapies and while historically much of  the challenge of brain metastases has  sort of been attributed to the  difficulty of penetrating the  blood-brain barrier there really is very  strong evidence now that brain  metastasis significantly disrupt the  blood-brain barrier and that there's  significant leakage of drugs into that  space indeed the BRF inhibitor dubrovnik  which was not predicted to cross the  blood-brain barrier based on preclinical  studies has actually very high response  rates in melanoma patients with brain  metastases and what's really instead  growing is an increasing appreciation  supported by data from multiple groups  showing that there can be unique  molecular and immune features in brain  metastases compared to non CNS  metastases and we've done some work in  this area our initial study back in 2009  was an experiment in which we looked at  Oct embedded or frozen melanoma  metastasis we had looked largely at  regional metastasis but we also did a  pilot study of small cohorts of distant  metastases receptive from the brain the  liver and the lung we used HD guided  macro