e same  patient and this actually has been  validated independently by a group in  Germany by immunohistochemistry studies  again strongly implicating the pi3  kinase pathway in brain metastasis now  again those studies were conducted with  OCT bedded frozen tissues and over time  again we've seen a rapid expansion in  the capabilities of multiple  technologies to  work with FFPE specimens which really  opens up large number of specimens for  analysis and so we've actually collected  a cohort of 98 brain metastases from 84  patients we've used these tissues to do  isolation of DNA and RNA the results of  those analyses will actually be  presented at the 2018 AAC our annual  meeting for those who've you are  interested in terms of proteins we've  done some standard IHC markers but again  this we thought was an interesting  opportunity to again use the naina  string digital spatial profiling  platform to characterize signaling  proteins and the immune response in  these clinical specimens and in  particular as Joe mentioned the ability  to use masking so that we could  separately look at the expression of  those markers in tumor cells and in  immune cells was something that again  was very attractive to us in these  highly heterogeneous specimens and so  this slide actually shows our first  pilot experiment this is actually a  brain metastasis that we analyzed from a  patient who had a brain metastasis and  an extra cranial metastasis we selected  now 12 regions of interest in each tumor  to assess spatial heterogeneity the data  was then that we obtained was normalized  to the number of cells in each ROI again  using the digital spatial profiling to  look at our panels of signaling pathway  proteins and what we again did is use  the masking and an example this is shown  down in the lower panel on the Left  where what we can see is we used s 100 B  to recognize the melanoma cells which  are shown in green we then used cd45  which is shown in red to recognize the  immune cells and then we could used a P  to actually count the number of nuclei  and so our initial analyses again wanted  to focus specifically on the status of  signaling pathways in the brain  metastasis and thus in the s 100  positive cells and so down here at the  bottom we see the results for the  signaling proteins in this single  patient where we again were able to  separately measure 12 different regions  of interest in the brain metastasis and  12 different regions of interest in the  extracranial metastasis  measure multiple phospho specific  proteins and as indicated by the arrows  what we saw for multiple markers in the  pi3 kinase pathway so that the  expression of these activation specific  markers was significantly higher in the  brain met from this patient which are  shown as the pink circles compared to  the non CNS metastases which are the  green circles there and so overall this  again sort of identified up regulation  of multiple phospho specific markers in  the pi3 kinase Akt pathway in brain  metastasis and in some ways again this  was a nice confirmation of what we had  seen before in our our PPA based study  but to me really sort of jumped out in  the fact that we were able to accomplish  this with a single FFPE slide the fact  that we were able to assess intramural  heterogeneity which we're still working  on and the fact that we again didn't  have to destroy any tissue so that we  could go on and do other studies on this  same slide and so shown here Joe  mentioned I think quite briefly but we  now are sort of working with manna  string on some pilot studies to again do  the digital spacial profiling but now  not of proteins but actually of RNA  molecules and so again this is being  accomplished on a single FFPE slide  where we're now doing in-situ spatial  analysis of just over a thousand rnas  where again we can use the masking  approach to separately look at the  expression in the tumor cells with the  s100 mask and separately and  independently in the immune cells that  we see in each of these tissue samples  using the cd45 mask and so this is again  a very exciting extension of the  technology that we think will be quite  powerful for our studies and studies of  other investigators moving forward and  so just to summarize the data again it's  really a very exciting time to be  working in oncology as we develop much  more effective therapies and as we  develop really powerful tools to really  sort of develop biomarkers that can be  used to guide the therapy for our  patients and so I hope again I've  explained that the fact that there is  really strong evidence and unk that  oncogenic pathways and tumor cells can  impact the anti tumor response and the  data that I didn't show you there's  actually very  strong data that the immune response  will impact the efficacy of targeted  therapy and so together this data really  strongly supports the rationale for  integrated analysis of both signaling  and immune pathways in our clinical  specimens I think hopefully you've also  appreciated the growing understanding  that we have of the complexity of and  the heterogeneity of both molecular de  Muniz that we can see in tissues both  between different tumors and even within  individual tumors and finally what we  find so exciting is the increasing  capabilities to interrogate molecular to  mewn features on small FFPE clinical  specimens and again as we've  demonstrated again a tremendous amount  of information that can be attained even  for single FFPE slides and I think this  again is providing tremendous potential  and opportunity for new insights new  biomarkers and ultimately new  personalized approaches for our patients  so with that I'd like to thank many of  the people who worked on this project  our collaborators and nana string and  the support that we had for the study  thank you  thank you dr. Michael Davis and dr.  Joseph Beecham that informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask the question  drop-down box located on the far left of  your presentation window type your  questions into the box that appears on  your screen and click on the send button  what answers many of your questions is  we have time for let's get started our  first question is for dr. Beecham what  do you see as the potential advantages  of digital spatial profiling  capabilities  thank you very much for the question I  think I think probably Mike Mike and I  actually sort of highlighted very very  similar things in terms of the intrinsic  advantages and I thought of one that we  actually did mention so I want to add  that to the to the list but clearly  being able to have the high multiplexing  you know allows Mike to be ask those  kinds of questions were what are the  signaling pathways linked to  heterogeneous areas of a tumor that's a  question that simply could not be a test  in a quantitative way before we were  able to do the kind of high multiplex  work that that the digital spatial  profiler provides so I think having the  high multiplex capability the unique  capability of being able to mask out  reaches up to her in tumor  micro-environment I think both of us  highlighted that this idea that you can  actually make these measurements and as  you actually haven't utilized any sample  so you get this reusability component  associated with the with the technology  is a huge advantage to be able to go and  be interrogate a sample so we're really  entering a new this is really a new type  of capability inside the microscope or  sort of bringing the power of molecular  profiling into the spatial regime and  that's really the major advance we have  here the one that we didn't mention so  much and I'm just going to highlight  here briefly is  you know microscopy is sort of a  specialized art you got people that know  how to run microscopes adult and I  should highlight this digital space your  profile you don't actually have to be a  microscope at all you really do is you  load the slides into the front of the of  the digital spatial profile you close  the door and then it's off it runs  automatically it's all remotely  controlled you could have a different it  could be being controlled by a  pathologist that's not even in your  building as we collaborate or it could  be you know halfway around the world and  actually be helping you control the  microscope or control the ahta me the  reaches of interest but the key thing is  you don't have to be on microscopy  expert to do this if you're a molecular  profiler and all of a sudden you really  want to become you know essentially  doing state-of-the-art high Plex  microscopy you don't have to learn how  to become a microscopy so all this  instrumentation is is basically  automated so I mean I think that that  captures the strategic features that  both Mike and I work we're trying to  highlight in this talk thank you yeah  yeah and I think I'll just add to it I  mean again as a biologist when we say  that tumors have increased expression of  a particular target versus another based  on homogenous we do always wonder is  this about sort of a specific sub  population of cells that have very high  expression whereas others don't or is  this something global and again really  appreciate with this technology the fact  that you can address this spatially and  actually not just for one marker but as  Joe mentioned really for whole panels of  markers at a time the other thing that  this actually allows us to do is  actually look at the interactions  between different markers between  different signaling pathways that I  think is really going to provide us with  much better granularity about our  understanding about the molecular  biology about the immunology and about  the heterogeneity of cancer  thank you our next question is for dr.  David you talk about the challenges  surrounding maximizing analysis of small  FFPE sections how does it platform like  dsb impact research strategies and your  everyday practices in the lab yeah  that's a it's a great question and again  I think one of the most interesting  aspects of the DSP platform you know  it's one of the things that in the  studies we presented we really were sort  of highlighting again looking at  heterogeneity of tumor cells again first  in regions with Inter terminal  heterogeneity and then with the brain  metastasis in tumors where we had a high  inter mixing of different cell  populations you know one of the  practical applications of this is  actually going to be is actually using  samples or biopsies where we actually  have a lot of necrosis and being able to  actually again not let those parts of  the tissue sort of contaminate your  analysis to be able to go in and  spatially pick out sort of regions of  viable tissue is another I think very  practical advantage of this approach I  think overall again what we're really  encouraged by is we as in the field of  melanoma have found DNA based predictors  of outcomes RNA based predictors of  outcomes and protein based predictors of  outcomes as I was illustrating at the  end the fact that we're you know  potentially looking at a platform where  you may be able to get DNA RNA and  protein based data really all at one  time is really quite intriguing  and I think you know in addition to  really sort of having potential  applications and research I think  ultimately I could have real  implications for you know clinical and  diagnostic testing and and hopefully for  guiding patient care  thank you for that answer our next  question is for dr. Davis how can the  DSP platform help to develop tools that  can be used to select patients that will  benefit from immunotherapy yeah so this  is obviously a very pressing question  not only in melanoma but in multiple  fields and again this is an area where  we've seen the development of both DNA  and RNA based biomarkers as well as some  a monistic chemical stains and again I  think that there's going to be an  opportunity to be able to assess these  things at the same time or to be able to  access multiple different parameters  again using a very small amount of FFPE  tissue I think the other part that what  we're going to see the ability to do is  again many types of approaches  particularly RNA based approaches have  been homogenous which really again end  up being significantly influenced by  what your relative proportion of tumor  cells and immune cells are I think where  there's a real opportunity here is to be  able to separately look to say are there  again markers in the tumor cells that  tell us about will patients respond or  not or separately  are there markers in those immune cells  and again one of the things that we're  particularly excited about is the  ability to look at the status of  signaling pathways not only in tumor  cells but at their status in the immune  cells as predictors of outcomes and our  hope is is that again doing those types  of analyses would not only identify  biomarkers that can predict which  patients will or won't respond but  hopefully we'll also provide biological  insights that explain why resistance  happens and again provides rationale  strategies to overcome them  thank you for that answer it looks like  we have time for one more question our  last question is for dr. David there is  a growing number of RNA based diagnostic  tests what are the advantages and  disadvantages of doing testing with  spatial resolution so great I think that  again sort of a very important question  again I think that one of the things  that's a real advantage of what we've  sort of described here so far is the  ability to do this with a single FFPE  slide the ability to know what it is  you're profiling and to do that quite  carefully I think that you know in terms  of disadvantages the part that we're  going to be moving forward with is  needing to validate those types of that  we're going to need to validate this  moving forward as a new way to look at  least when we talk about things like RNA  signatures certainly again I think in  terms of looking at protein markers like  cd8 in cd8 positive cell infiltrates or  PDL one expression those are going to  convert very easily but I think that  again this is a very powerful approach  that we're going to be able to use  moving forward  I'd like to make one more comment there  so I am sitting here looking at the last  slide here and I wanted to call out  thanks to one other person that actually  I see on your acknowledgments there Mike  could you don't mind  you know both Mike and I work very  closely with AG uh the professor down  there named Gordon Mills and I just when  I highlight and thank Gordon actually  Gordon and I are sort of co-inventors of  this particular microscope and I just  want to sort of highlight and thank  Gordon died here publicly so that no  it's been a really fun project and  that's and working with Mike it's been a  close relationship between all the  protein detection Singh and the whole  team at MD Anderson is just exceptional  and it's been fun Co developing this  technology together sort of technology  again commercial technology developing  company with the best medical center in  the world and I think I think together  we come up with a pretty nice tool  that's going to help not patient care in  the long term  and it's been a pleasure to again work  on this and again particularly to work  with people are so invested again in  generating a project that will really  help patients and again I think this is  a win-win for everyone  I would like to once again thank dr.  Michael Davis and dr. Joseph Beecham for  their presentation I would also like to  thank nano string technologies for  making today's educational webcast  possible before we go I want to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing 